-Remote monitoring of small-scale wind energy systems requires integrating physical variable acquisition with secure communication, data storage and querying. Implementing this data path requires coordinating embedded device programming with cloud service configuration. This work addresses that need by developing and validating an architecture based on ESP32-C6 devices and AWS services, documenting the connections between its components and the tests used to verify their operation.
+Remote monitoring of small wind systems requires integrating data acquisition, secure communication, storage and querying. In the prototype studied, this requires coordinating embedded programming with cloud configuration. This work develops an architecture using ESP32-C6 devices and AWS and documents its integration and functional tests.
 
 The acquisition system is organized into three stages: a field node that measures generator variables, a local integration node that consolidates environmental and operational variables and structures the telemetry message, and an IoT gateway responsible for Wi-Fi connectivity, the secure MQTT session and data publication to the cloud.
 
-In the cloud layer, the architecture uses AWS IoT Core for MQTTs ingestion, DynamoDB to keep the latest value per device, S3 as the historical repository, Glue and Athena to catalog and query time series, Lambda as a serverless API, and a React/Vite dashboard served through S3 and CloudFront. This separation distinguishes the near-real-time data path from the historical path, reducing costs and facilitating scalability.
-
-The main contribution is a reproducible methodology to instrument, transmit and analyze data from small wind systems by integrating IoT, cloud computing and Big Data, with traceability from sensors and firmware to web visualization and experimental validation.
+In the cloud layer, the architecture uses AWS IoT Core for MQTTs ingestion, DynamoDB to keep the latest value per device, S3 as the historical repository, Glue and Athena to catalog and query time series, Lambda as a serverless API, and a React/Vite dashboard served through S3 and CloudFront. Validation involved one turbine: 7,546 S3 records were recovered over 24 hours, with intervals of 11 or 12 seconds. AWS reported an account-wide cost of USD 0.106640 for the two days covering that window. Multi-turbine operation and monthly costs are projections without experimental validation.
+
+The main contribution is a documented implementation to instrument, transmit and analyze data from small wind systems by integrating IoT, cloud computing and Big Data, with traceability from sensors and firmware to web visualization and experimental validation.
 
